# Cursor Editor — Automations Editor

## Overview

Cursor Automations is a visual editor that lets you define agent workflows triggered by external events. Unlike hooks (which run shell scripts or prompt-based checks on Cursor events), Automations create **cloud agent runs** triggered by events from integrated services such as GitHub, Slack, Linear, PagerDuty, Sentry, and cron schedulers.

Automations run on Cursor's cloud infrastructure (Cloud Agent), not locally. Each automation consists of a trigger, a set of tools, instructions (prompt), and model configuration.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Trigger Event] --> B[Cloud Agent Starts]
    B --> C[Agent Executes Tools]
    C --> D[Follow Prompt Instructions]
    D --> E[Post Results]
    E --> F[Slack / PR Comment / etc.]
```

## How Automations Differ from Hooks

| Aspect | Hooks (`hooks.json`) | Automations Editor |
|---------|---------------------|-------------------|
| **Execution location** | Local (runs on your machine) | Cloud (Cursor's infrastructure) |
| **Trigger source** | Cursor internal events | External services (Git, Slack, PagerDuty, etc.) |
| **Output** | JSON to stdin/stdout | Agent runs with tool actions |
| **Language** | Shell scripts / prompt-based | Visual editor with drag-and-drop |
| **Agent type** | No agent (script execution) | Full Claude Code agent |
| **Use case** | Pre/post execution gates, formatting, validation | Scheduled tasks, incident response, code review |

---

## Automations Editor Interface

The Automations editor is accessible through Cursor's UI. It provides a visual workflow builder with:

- **Trigger configuration**: Select event type and scope
- **Tool selection**: Choose what the agent can do (post to Slack, comment on PR, etc.)
- **Prompt/instructions editor**: Define agent behavior when the trigger fires
- **Model selection**: Choose the AI model for the agent
- **Cloud Agent settings**: Configure agent options, memory, and compute settings

### Accessing the Editor

The editor is opened via the `cursor-app-control.open_automation` tool when available, or through the Cursor Automations UI panel.

